     (description . "Fixed the reader/printer asymmetry documented in tests/lisp15-library-functions/PROVENANCE.md: the printer has long produced dotted-pair notation (A . B) on output, but the reader could not parse the same syntax back on input -- a standalone . token was read literally as a symbol, not recognized as the pair separator. Added a new peek_dot helper, called from read_list between reading the car element and deciding whether to recurse for a proper list or read exactly one more expression as the cdr. Real finding: the bug was masked by a visual coincidence -- (QUOTE (A . B)) on the old reader read as an ordinary 3-element list (A, the symbol ., and B), and printing that 3-element list through the plain space-separated list printer happened to produce the exact same string, (A . B), as the true dotted-pair notation for a 2-element cons -- so naive round-trip tests couldn't see the bug at all; only structural checks (CDR giving (. B) instead of B) reveal it. New corpus tests/reader-dotted-pairs/ (6 fixtures, 4 of which genuinely discriminate old/new behavior -- honestly documented which 2 don't and why), wired into tests/closeout/run.sh as a ninth gate. Adversarially confirmed against the pre-change kernel.")
