   ("APPENDIX-B-PROPLISTS-1" .
    ((priority . 9.1)
     (capabilities . (assembly x86-64 property-lists historical-provenance regression-testing))
     (origin . owner)
     (context . "Owner picked items 3 and 4 from a status update (\"3-4\"): reader dot-notation (previous entry) and property-list functions -- explicitly noted earlier as not requiring PROG or closures and technically reachable with the same approach already used throughout this session.")
     (description . "Read the actual LISP 1.5 Manual (1962) directly (\"7.3 Property Lists\", ст.39-41, and the GET/PUTPROP-equivalent function definitions, ст.58-59, both via direct page-image reads). Found that PUTPROP -- the name a later Lisp dialect (MacLisp/Lisp 1.6) uses for this -- does not exist anywhere in this 1962 manual (confirmed via full-text search, zero matches); the real 1962 function with the same purpose is called DEFLIST. Implemented GET, DEFLIST, and REMPROP as ordinary evaluated-argument primitives (same tier as RPLACA/RPLACD), backed by a new proplist_table global array, index-parallel to symtab, giving every symbol its own independent property list -- a flat, alternating (indicator value indicator value ...) list, matching the manual's own NIL/FF property-list diagrams exactly. Deliberately does not model the real system's \"-1 sentinel head\" or its internal PNAME/EXPR/SUBR indicators (a 704/CTSS-specific representation detail this kernel doesn't share, since functions here live in global_env via DEFINE, not on property lists) -- only the general, user-visible GET/DEFLIST/REMPROP facility itself. Real finding during verification: the scanned image of GET's own recursive step is ambiguous -- it appears to use a single cdr, which cannot be structurally correct for a flat alternating list (the very next car would be a value being compared as an indicator). Resolved via internal cross-check against the neighboring prop[x;y;u] formula on the same page, which correctly uses a single cdr for its own, different, one-step list scan -- confirming the image itself renders reliably, so GET's step is either a scan/resolution artifact or a genuine manual typo; implemented as get[cddr[x];y], the only structurally consistent reading, explicitly labeled as a reconstruction-derived corrected reading rather than a blind transcription. DEFLIST prepends new indicator/value pairs onto the front of a symbol's plist (\"puts things on at the front\", ст.58), so a repeated indicator is shadowed rather than physically replaced -- matching \"the old value will be replaced by the new one\" as an observable effect of GET always finding the front-most match; returns the list of u's, consistent with define[x] = deflist[x;EXPR] needing the same return shape. REMPROP rebuilds a filtered list removing ALL occurrences of an indicator, rather than the real system's literal RPLACD splice -- same observable result. New corpus tests/appendix-b-proplists/ (7 fixtures), wired into tests/closeout/run.sh as a tenth gate. Adversarially confirmed against the pre-change kernel: 6 of 7 fixtures genuinely fail on it; 1 honestly doesn't discriminate (correct answer NIL coincides with the old kernel's unrelated UNBOUND-then-NIL fallback), documented rather than hidden.")
     (done . "2026-09-22: GET/DEFLIST/REMPROP implemented per the manual's own property-list section (ст.39-41, 58-59), a scanned-image ambiguity in GET's own formula resolved via cross-check with a neighboring formula, adversarially confirmed, tenth closeout gate green.")))
   ("APPENDIX-B-FLAG-REMFLAG-1" .
    ((priority . 9.0)
     (capabilities . (assembly x86-64 property-lists historical-provenance regression-testing self-correction))
     (origin . owner)
     (context . "Owner asked \"чому поза\" (why out of scope) about arrays and flag/remflag, having been told they were excluded by analogy with LAP. Owner then asked to implement flag/remflag (cheap) and/or ARRAY/STORE, after this agent admitted the LAP-analogy exclusion was not actually justified for either.")
     (description . "While reasoning through how to implement flag/remflag correctly (ст.59: \"an indicator on a property list that does not have a property following it is called a flag\"), found that the immediately preceding APPENDIX-B-PROPLISTS-1 entry's own GET fix was itself wrong. That entry had \"corrected\" GET's scanned single-cdr recursive step to a double-cdr (cddr) step, reasoning that a single step couldn't be structurally correct for a flat alternating (indicator value) list. This reasoning silently assumed every property-list entry is a strict 2-element pair -- which a flag (a bare, valueless indicator) breaks by definition. A single-cdr scan is actually the one that correctly generalizes to lists mixing flags and pairs (it just walks one cell at a time, finds the first occurrence of the target indicator, and returns whatever follows it) -- the cddr version corrupts alignment the moment a flag appears anywhere before the target. Reverted GET to the literally-scanned get[cdr[x];y]; all 7 existing fixtures re-verified unchanged (none had mixed flags into a plist, which is why the bug went unnoticed through PR #49's own review and adversarial pass). Implemented flag[l;ind] (prepends a bare indicator onto every symbol's plist in list l, unless already present -- \"no property list ever receives a duplicated flag\") and remflag[l;ind] (removes all occurrences, one element per match, deliberately different from remprop's two-element-per-match removal -- applying remprop to a flag would wrongly eat the next real indicator as if it were the flag's own value). Added 3 new fixtures to tests/appendix-b-proplists/ (now 10 total): one proving GET correctly skips a flag mixed in front of a real pair (a direct regression witness for the cdr/cddr fix), one proving flag's no-duplicate rule, one proving remflag surgically removes only the flag without corrupting a neighboring pair. Adversarially confirmed against HEAD (PR #49's own committed state, which already had working GET/DEFLIST/REMPROP but the cddr bug and no FLAG/REMFLAG at all): 2 of the 3 new fixtures genuinely discriminate; the third honestly doesn't (FLAG's own absence and its correct no-duplicate behavior here produce the same visible NIL for opposite reasons -- documented, not hidden). docs/S0-SEED-CONTRACT.md and README.md updated with the full self-correction writeup, not just the new feature; symbol count now 57.")
     (done . "2026-09-22: found and fixed a real, self-introduced bug (GET's cddr vs cdr) from the immediately preceding entry, before it could cause a silent wrong answer on any plist mixing flags with pairs; flag/remflag implemented and adversarially confirmed; tenth gate (appendix-b-proplists) still green with 10 fixtures.")))
